{
  "id": "https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/contraception",
  "type": "Concept",
  "prefLabel": {
    "en": "contraception"
  },
  "definition": {
    "en": "A set of reversible or permanent methods, measures, and practices aimed at preventing pregnancy, the choice of which should be based on reliable information, voluntariness, and informed consent."
  },
  "scopeNote": {
    "en": [
      "WHO considers access to contraception an element of sexual and reproductive health and of the right to decide freely and responsibly on the number and spacing of children. In the case of girls and women with disabilities, contraception should also be analysed in the context of cultural norms concerning sexuality, gender, and motherhood, as well as stereotypes portraying them as asexual, incapable of parenthood, or in need of reproductive control. Research identifies communication, architectural, and institutional barriers, as well as staff attitudes, that may limit genuine choice of contraceptive methods. Contraception can be analysed through knowledge of and access to methods, their use, freedom of choice, quality of counselling, availability of services, consent and decision-making autonomy, and experiences of pressure, refusal, or coercion."
    ]
  },
  "notation": [
    "11.2"
  ],
  "broader": {
    "id": "https://m-lancini.github.io/frida-vocab/dictionary/section-11",
    "prefLabel": {
      "en": "Sexual and reproductive health and rights"
    }
  },
